  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 1232 AM MDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nThursday...All indications point to Thursday as seeing the greatest \nimpacts from this Pacific storm. As the low continues moving east \nacross CA and NV through the day, an upper shortwave rotates across \nAZ and into the Four Corners region. This upper support, coupled \nwith increasing proximity of the system, will mean periods of heavy \nrainfall across western CO and along the Continental Divide through \nthe entire day. The focus area continues to be the southwest mts, \nwith total additional rainfall amounts ranging from an inch up to \n2.75 inches possible through the day, while the remainder of the \nhigher terrain could see around half an inch. SPC shows the Marginal \narea for strong to severe thunderstorms creeping into the \nsouthwestern corner of the state, giving a nod to the abundant \nmoisture plus upper level support, so periods of heavy convective \nrainfall falling on already saturated ground and some high elevation \nsnow pack leading to excessive runoff will be possible, hence the \nongoing flood Watch. Those living or traveling across the southwest \nmountains should stay weather aware through the rest of the week, \nand have multiple ways to receive weather warnings. As for the \neastern plains, dry and continued warm conditions anticipated, \nthough the upper ridge axis finally pushes east out of the area. \nLook for highs in the 60s for the high valleys, and 70s to lower 80s \nfor the plains.\n\nFriday...Friday will be an interesting day as the system starts to \nweaken and split into two pieces by the evening. The northern piece \npushes up into MT, while the southern piece drops south and tries to \ngather some strength over the Desert SW. Rain totals of an \nadditional .40 of an inch for the eastern San Juans, and up to a \ntenth of an inch elsewhere, are forecast, then showers and isolated \nthunderstorms push along the southern border and up across the far \nsoutheast corner through the late afternoon and evening. This will \nbe the last really warm day for the eastern plains as temps once \nagain climb into the 70s to around 80F, but there are some hints \nthat perhaps it might end up a few degrees cooler due to increased \ncloud cover.\n\nSaturday through Tuesday...While one low center lingers across the \nFour Corners, the northern piece cross the northern Rockies and \npushes a cold front south into eastern CO Saturday morning. This \nfront will bring significant cooling to the forecast area as well as \nwidespread precipitation chances for both Sat and Sun as the \nsouthern piece of the low weakens and finally moves east across the \nstate. Long range models indicate that Mon and Tue will remain \nunsettled and cool across the region, but confidence remains low on \nwhat that actually looks like. For now, the NBM drops temps into the \n50s and 60s on Sat, 50s regionwide Sun and Mon, then some gradual \nwarming by Tue.",
